// Terry Andrew Davis (1969 - 2018)
// Sole developer of TempleOS — a 64-bit operating system
// written entirely by one man over roughly 10 years.
//
// 100,000+ lines of code. Alone.
// His own compiler. His own kernel. His own filesystem.
// His own programming language: HolyC.
//
// He believed TempleOS was a temple for God.
// Dimensions of the screen: 640x480, as commanded.
// No internet. No networking. By design.
// A computer as a sacred space.
//
// He was a genius. He was also schizophrenic.
// The world was not kind to him.
// He lived in a van near the end.
// He was struck by a train in The Dalles, Oregon.
//
// But TempleOS runs. Right now. Somewhere.
// And HolyC is still the most personal programming
// language ever written — because it was written
// for an audience of exactly one, plus God.
//
// RIP Terry.
// The compiler worked. It always worked.

U0 Terry()
{
  "It's quite simple. God said 640x480 16 colors.\n";
  "I am the smartest programmer that has ever lived.\n";
  "I do feel called by God to do this.\n";
  "An operating system is a work of art.\n";
}

Terry;
